WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.507 --> 00:00:17.354
For many of us right now,
our lives are quieter than normal.

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:20.428
And quiet can be unnerving.

00:00:20.849 --> 00:00:22.374
It can make you feel lonely,

00:00:22.398 --> 00:00:25.911
or just all too aware
of the things you're missing out on.

00:00:26.427 --> 00:00:28.627
I think about sound all the time.

00:00:28.934 --> 00:00:30.117
I'm a sound designer,

00:00:30.141 --> 00:00:32.426
and I host the podcast
"Twenty Thousand Hertz."

00:00:33.109 --> 00:00:36.803
It's all about the world's most
recognizable and interesting sounds.

00:00:37.196 --> 00:00:40.608
But I think this is the perfect time
to talk about silence.

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:42.800
Because what I've come to understand

00:00:42.824 --> 00:00:46.666
is that there is no such thing as silence.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.236
And the person who opened
my mind to this idea

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.335
is one of the most influential
composers in history.

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:55.296
(Piano music)

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.915
John Cage has made an impact
on artists in many genres,

00:00:58.939 --> 00:01:02.740
from avant-garde musicians,
to modern dance, to pop music.

00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:05.426
Right now, we're listening
to his 1948 piece

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:06.831
called "In a Landscape."

00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:10.387
This version was recorded in 1994
by Stephen Drury.

00:01:10.411 --> 00:01:16.768
(Piano music)

00:01:16.792 --> 00:01:20.643
This piece is actually not very typical
of John Cage's writing.

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:24.182
He's more known for his innovations
and avant-garde techniques.

00:01:24.206 --> 00:01:26.206
But despite his reputation,

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:30.752
no one was prepared
for what he did in 1952,

00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:34.309
when he created the most daring
piece of his career.

00:01:34.657 --> 00:01:38.147
It was called "4'33'',"

00:01:38.171 --> 00:01:43.299
and it was a piece that some critics
even refused to call "music,"

00:01:43.323 --> 00:01:45.887
because for the entire
duration of the piece,

00:01:45.911 --> 00:01:47.100
the performer plays

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:49.434
nothing at all.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.807
Well, to be technical,
the performer is actually playing rest.

00:01:53.831 --> 00:01:56.942
But to the audience,
it looks like nothing is happening.

00:01:57.601 --> 00:02:00.299
John Cage's "4'33''"
was performed for the first time

00:02:00.323 --> 00:02:02.227
in the summer of 1952,

00:02:02.251 --> 00:02:04.363
by renowned pianist David Tudor.

00:02:04.673 --> 00:02:07.582
It was at the Maverick Concert hall
in Woodstock, New York.

00:02:07.606 --> 00:02:11.656
This is a beautiful wooden building
with huge openings to the outdoors.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.903
So, David Tudor walked out on stage,

00:02:13.927 --> 00:02:15.491
sat down at the piano,

00:02:15.515 --> 00:02:17.443
then closed the piano lid.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.274
He then sat in silence,

00:02:19.298 --> 00:02:21.814
only moving to open
and close the piano lid

00:02:21.838 --> 00:02:24.433
between each of the three movements.

00:02:24.457 --> 00:02:25.790
After the time was up,

00:02:25.814 --> 00:02:26.981
he got up

00:02:27.005 --> 00:02:28.671
and walked off the stage.

00:02:29.537 --> 00:02:30.981
(Piano music)

00:02:31.005 --> 00:02:33.598
The audience had no idea what to think.

00:02:34.037 --> 00:02:38.101
It made people wonder if Cage
is even taking his career seriously.

00:02:38.125 --> 00:02:39.855
A close friend even wrote to him,

00:02:39.879 --> 00:02:43.260
begging that he not turn
his career into a joke.

00:02:43.284 --> 00:02:45.601
John Cage had, well, if you could call it,

00:02:45.625 --> 00:02:47.293
composed a piece of music

00:02:47.317 --> 00:02:50.761
that really challenged
some very established ideas

00:02:50.785 --> 00:02:52.313
about music composition.

00:02:52.337 --> 00:02:55.105
It's something that musicians
still debate today.

00:02:56.255 --> 00:02:58.786
To understand just what
John Cage was thinking,

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.588
let's back up to the 1940s.

00:03:00.612 --> 00:03:01.769
Back then,

00:03:01.793 --> 00:03:05.841
John Cage was making a name for himself
composing for the prepared piano.

00:03:05.865 --> 00:03:07.077
(Piano music)

00:03:07.101 --> 00:03:08.315
To make music like this,

00:03:08.339 --> 00:03:11.125
John Cage would put objects
inside the piano,

00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:12.482
between the strings.

00:03:12.784 --> 00:03:14.450
Things you just find lying around,

00:03:14.474 --> 00:03:17.505
like screws, tape and rubber erasers.

00:03:17.831 --> 00:03:20.077
So now, you've transformed the piano

00:03:20.101 --> 00:03:22.934
from a tonal instrument
with high and low pitches

00:03:22.958 --> 00:03:25.497
into a collection of unique sounds.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.561
The music you're hearing 
is Cage's "Sonata V,"

00:03:28.585 --> 00:03:30.997
from "Sonatas and Interludes
for Prepared Piano."

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.161
Probably his most famous work
outside of "4'33''."

00:03:34.703 --> 00:03:37.029
This version was performed
by Boris Berman.

00:03:37.997 --> 00:03:40.752
John Cage wrote incredibly
detailed instructions

00:03:40.776 --> 00:03:43.593
about where to place
each object in the piano.

00:03:43.617 --> 00:03:47.864
But it's impossible for every performer
to get the exact same objects,

00:03:47.888 --> 00:03:50.387
so the sound you get is always different.

00:03:50.411 --> 00:03:53.148
Basically, it comes down to random chance.

00:03:53.458 --> 00:03:55.918
This was pretty bananas and pretty alien

00:03:55.942 --> 00:03:59.767
to the way most composers and musicians
are taught to do things.

00:04:00.927 --> 00:04:03.458
John Cage was becoming
increasingly interested

00:04:03.482 --> 00:04:05.664
in chance and randomness

00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:08.498
and letting the universe
provide the answer to the question

00:04:08.522 --> 00:04:10.588
"What note should I play next?"

00:04:11.212 --> 00:04:13.418
But to hear the answer to the question,

00:04:13.442 --> 00:04:15.243
first, you have to listen.

00:04:15.561 --> 00:04:17.014
And in the 1940s,

00:04:17.038 --> 00:04:20.037
listening to the universe
was getting harder to do.

00:04:20.458 --> 00:04:22.283
(Elevator music)

00:04:22.307 --> 00:04:24.641
The Muzak company was founded in the '30s.

00:04:24.665 --> 00:04:25.855
It really took off,

00:04:25.879 --> 00:04:29.363
and soon, there was constant
background music nearly everywhere.

00:04:29.911 --> 00:04:31.932
It was almost impossible to escape.

00:04:32.584 --> 00:04:35.707
John Cage realized
that people were losing the option

00:04:35.731 --> 00:04:38.091
to shut out the background
music of the world.

00:04:38.457 --> 00:04:42.337
He worried that Muzak would prevent people
from hearing silence altogether.

00:04:43.454 --> 00:04:44.755
In 1948,

00:04:44.779 --> 00:04:47.438
four years before he wrote "4'33'',"

00:04:47.462 --> 00:04:49.492
John Cage mentioned
that he wanted to write

00:04:49.516 --> 00:04:52.025
a four-and-a-half-minute-long
piece of silence

00:04:52.049 --> 00:04:54.461
and sell it to the Muzak company.

00:04:54.485 --> 00:04:56.802
It started as something
of a political statement

00:04:56.826 --> 00:04:58.795
or an offhand comment,

00:04:58.819 --> 00:05:01.794
but this idea struck a nerve
and quickly evolved.

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:05.457
John Cage was starting to think
deeply about silence.

00:05:05.981 --> 00:05:09.314
And when he visited a truly quiet place,

00:05:09.338 --> 00:05:11.338
he made a startling discovery.

00:05:12.195 --> 00:05:16.354
John Cage visited an anechoic chamber
at Harvard University.

00:05:16.378 --> 00:05:19.078
Anechoic chambers are rooms
that are acoustically treated

00:05:19.102 --> 00:05:21.553
to minimize sound to almost zero.

00:05:21.911 --> 00:05:23.712
There are no sounds in these rooms,

00:05:23.736 --> 00:05:26.587
so John Cage didn't expect
to hear anything at all.

00:05:27.101 --> 00:05:30.147
But he actually heard
his own blood circulating.

00:05:30.171 --> 00:05:31.851
(Pulse)

00:05:31.875 --> 00:05:34.133
I've personally experienced
an anechoic chamber,

00:05:34.157 --> 00:05:36.117
and it's a really wild experience

00:05:36.141 --> 00:05:38.410
that can completely change
your perceptions

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:40.574
about sound and silence.

00:05:40.926 --> 00:05:43.974
It really felt like my brain
just turning up an amplifier,

00:05:43.998 --> 00:05:46.149
grasping for anything to hear.

00:05:46.575 --> 00:05:47.853
Just like John Cage,

00:05:47.877 --> 00:05:51.821
I could very clearly hear my blood
pushing through my body.

00:05:51.845 --> 00:05:54.191
John Cage realized, in that moment,

00:05:54.215 --> 00:05:58.350
that no matter where we are,
even our bodies are making sound.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:02.544
There's basically no such thing
as true silence.

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:05.044
As long as you are in your body,

00:06:05.068 --> 00:06:07.059
you're always hearing something.

00:06:07.538 --> 00:06:10.561
This is where John Cage's interest
in chance and randomness

00:06:10.585 --> 00:06:12.847
met his interest in silence.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.627
He realized that creating an environment
with no distractions

00:06:16.651 --> 00:06:18.704
wasn't about creating silence.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.753
It wasn't even about controlling noise.

00:06:22.149 --> 00:06:25.165
It was about the sounds
that were already there,

00:06:25.189 --> 00:06:27.673
but you suddenly hear for the first time

00:06:27.697 --> 00:06:30.030
when you're really ready to listen.

00:06:31.204 --> 00:06:34.355
That's what's so often
misunderstood about "4'33''."

00:06:34.379 --> 00:06:36.227
People assume it's a joke,

00:06:36.251 --> 00:06:38.895
but that couldn't be further
from the truth.

00:06:38.919 --> 00:06:41.212
It sounds different
everywhere you play it.

00:06:41.236 --> 00:06:42.887
And that's the point.

00:06:42.911 --> 00:06:45.752
What John Cage really wanted us to hear

00:06:45.776 --> 00:06:48.848
is the beauty of the sonic
world around us.

00:06:48.872 --> 00:06:52.124
(Birds chirping)

00:06:52.785 --> 00:06:57.166
(Overlapping voices)

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:01.037
(Church bell ringing)

00:07:01.061 --> 00:07:05.227
(Crickets chirping and owl hooting)

00:07:05.982 --> 00:07:08.339
"4'33''" should be a mindful experience

00:07:08.363 --> 00:07:12.264
that helps you focus on accepting things
just the way they are.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:16.629
It's not something that anyone else
can tell you how you're supposed to feel.

00:07:16.653 --> 00:07:18.053
It's deeply personal.

00:07:18.542 --> 00:07:20.915
It also brings up
some pretty big questions

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:22.505
about our sonic world.

00:07:22.831 --> 00:07:25.362
Is "4'33''" music, is it sound,

00:07:25.386 --> 00:07:26.879
is sound music?

00:07:26.903 --> 00:07:28.545
Is there even a difference?

00:07:28.919 --> 00:07:30.497
John Cage reminds us

00:07:30.521 --> 00:07:34.235
that music isn't the only kind of sound
worth listening to.

00:07:34.546 --> 00:07:36.946
All sounds are worth thinking about.

00:07:37.339 --> 00:07:39.717
We have a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity

00:07:39.741 --> 00:07:41.513
to reset our ears.

00:07:41.839 --> 00:07:44.609
And if we become more conscious
of what we hear,

00:07:44.633 --> 00:07:47.402
we'll inherently make
our world sound better.

00:07:48.149 --> 00:07:52.117
Quietness is not when we turn off
our minds to sound,

00:07:52.585 --> 00:07:54.589
but when we can really start to listen

00:07:54.613 --> 00:07:57.746
and hear the world
in all of its sonic beauty.

00:07:58.184 --> 00:07:59.807
So in this spirit,

00:07:59.831 --> 00:08:02.418
let's perform "4'33''" together,

00:08:02.442 --> 00:08:03.974
wherever you are.

00:08:03.998 --> 00:08:05.156
It's three movements,

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:07.014
and I'll let you know when they start.

00:08:07.038 --> 00:08:10.650
Listen to the texture and rhythm
of the sounds around you right now.

00:08:11.037 --> 00:08:12.664
Listen for the loud and soft,

00:08:12.688 --> 00:08:14.498
the harmonic, the dissonant,

00:08:14.522 --> 00:08:18.331
and all the small details
that make every sound unique.

00:08:18.668 --> 00:08:23.831
Spend this time as mindful and focused
in this real-life sonic moment.

00:08:24.279 --> 00:08:27.831
Enjoy the magnificence
of hearing and listening.

00:08:28.331 --> 00:08:30.264
So here comes the first movement.

00:08:30.288 --> 00:08:31.621
Starting ...

00:08:31.645 --> 00:08:33.038
now.

00:08:33.062 --> 00:08:34.213
[I. Tacet]

00:08:34.237 --> 00:08:35.387
(No audio)

00:09:02.158 --> 00:09:03.758
And here's movement two.

00:09:03.782 --> 00:09:06.315
It will be two minutes and 23 seconds.

00:09:06.952 --> 00:09:08.103
[II. Tacet]

00:09:08.127 --> 00:09:09.277
(No audio)

00:11:29.844 --> 00:11:31.577
And here is the final movement.

00:11:31.601 --> 00:11:34.068
It will be one minute and 40 seconds.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:36.241
[III. Tacet]

00:11:36.265 --> 00:11:37.415
(No audio)

00:13:15.214 --> 00:13:16.412
And that's it.

00:13:16.436 --> 00:13:17.586
We did it.

00:13:18.119 --> 00:13:19.519
Thanks for listening.

